As long as the price does not consolidate above the resistance level, there is a possibility of a change in trend and the start of a downward trend.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title>Gold Broke the Structure, but the Macro Pressure Is Easing</title><link>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/XAUUSD/dZobXVuZ-Gold-Broke-the-Structure-but-the-Macro-Pressure-Is-Easing/</link><description>Gold has lost the structure that supported most of the August rally.

The rising trendline is broken, and the 4,410–4,460 area has also failed. Price is now trying to recover from around 4,380, but for the moment that former support sits above the market as resistance.

The interesting part is that today’s macro backdrop is already a little less hostile.

Gold rebounded more than 1% after Treasury yields and the dollar pulled back from their recent highs. U.S. private payroll growth also came in softer than expected, which took some pressure off the rates story.

But I would not call the Fed problem solved.

Markets are still pricing roughly a 64% chance of a rate hike this month. That is high enough to keep real yields and the dollar relevant for gold, especially after such a sharp rally in August.

What the chart shows

The technical damage is fairly clear.

Gold broke the rising support line and then failed to hold the 4,410–4,460 zone. Unless price can reclaim that area, the short-term structure remains weaker.

The next major level below is around 4,205–4,230.

That zone matters because it was the base of the previous breakout. A move there would be a much deeper correction, but not necessarily the end of the larger recovery.

Primary scenario

The cautious view remains valid while gold stays below 4,410–4,460.

If rebounds continue to fail beneath that area, a deeper retest toward 4,205–4,230 remains credible.

Alternative scenario

The picture improves if buyers reclaim 4,460 and start holding above it again.

That would suggest the breakdown was more about a temporary rates shock than a full reversal in gold’s larger structure.

What comes next

Friday’s U.S. jobs report is the obvious catalyst.

A weak number could push Treasury yields lower again and challenge current Fed hike expectations. A stronger print would make the broken gold structure harder to repair.

Gold has lost the technical support that carried the rally, but the macro pressure behind the selloff is no longer moving in one direction.</description><pubDate>Wed, 02 Sep 2026 19:36:17 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/XAUUSD/dZobXVuZ-Gold-Broke-the-Structure-but-the-Macro-Pressure-Is-Easing/</guid><content:encoded><![CDATA[
<div class="avatar">
	<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/u/Evelyn_Reed/" title="User Profile" target="_blank">
		<img src="https://s3.tradingview.com/userpics/144937190-8oXr_mid.png" width="32" height="32"/>
	</a>
</div>
<div class="chart-name">
	<h1>Gold Broke the Structure, but the Macro Pressure Is Easing</h1>
</div>

<div class="chart-symbol">
<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/symbols/XAUUSD/">Gold</a>	<span class="symbol-descr">OANDA:XAUUSD</span>
</div>
<div class="chart-author">
	<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/u/Evelyn_Reed/" title="User Profile" target="_blank">Evelyn_Reed</a>
</div>

<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/chart/XAUUSD/dZobXVuZ-Gold-Broke-the-Structure-but-the-Macro-Pressure-Is-Easing/">
	<img src="https://s3.tradingview.com/d/dZobXVuZ_mid.png"/>
</a>
<p>Gold has lost the structure that supported most of the August rally. <br/>
 <br/>
The rising trendline is broken, and the 4,410–4,460 area has also failed. Price is now trying to recover from around 4,380, but for the moment that former support sits above the market as resistance. <br/>
 <br/>
The interesting part is that today’s macro backdrop is already a little less hostile. <br/>
 <br/>
Gold rebounded more than 1% after Treasury yields and the dollar pulled back from their recent highs. U.S. private payroll growth also came in softer than expected, which took some pressure off the rates story. <br/>
 <br/>
But I would not call the Fed problem solved. <br/>
 <br/>
Markets are still pricing roughly a 64% chance of a rate hike this month. That is high enough to keep real yields and the dollar relevant for gold, especially after such a sharp rally in August. <br/>
 <br/>
What the chart shows <br/>
 <br/>
The technical damage is fairly clear. <br/>
 <br/>
Gold broke the rising support line and then failed to hold the 4,410–4,460 zone. Unless price can reclaim that area, the short-term structure remains weaker. <br/>
 <br/>
The next major level below is around 4,205–4,230. <br/>
 <br/>
That zone matters because it was the base of the previous breakout. A move there would be a much deeper correction, but not necessarily the end of the larger recovery. <br/>
 <br/>
Primary scenario <br/>
 <br/>
The cautious view remains valid while gold stays below 4,410–4,460. <br/>
 <br/>
If rebounds continue to fail beneath that area, a deeper retest toward 4,205–4,230 remains credible. <br/>
 <br/>
Alternative scenario <br/>
 <br/>
The picture improves if buyers reclaim 4,460 and start holding above it again. <br/>
 <br/>
That would suggest the breakdown was more about a temporary rates shock than a full reversal in gold’s larger structure. <br/>
 <br/>
What comes next <br/>
 <br/>
Friday’s U.S. jobs report is the obvious catalyst. <br/>
 <br/>
A weak number could push Treasury yields lower again and challenge current Fed hike expectations. A stronger print would make the broken gold structure harder to repair. <br/>
 <br/>
Gold has lost the technical support that carried the rally, but the macro pressure behind the selloff is no longer moving in one direction.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title>uptrend </title><link>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/US30/gkyMt2Z5-uptrend/</link><description>The current uptrend is expected to continue to the specified resistance level. Then, given the behavior of the index in the resistance level, the continuation of the trend will be according to the specified paths.</description><pubDate>Wed, 02 Sep 2026 19:35:46 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/US30/gkyMt2Z5-uptrend/</guid><content:encoded><![CDATA[
