Within the specified support levels, there is a possibility of a trend change.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title>EUR/USD Lost the Breakout, but the ECB Is Getting More Hawkish</title><link>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/EURUSD/DM8T78iQ-EUR-USD-Lost-the-Breakout-but-the-ECB-Is-Getting-More-Hawkish/</link><description>EUR/USD is back below the 1.161–1.162 area after failing to hold the August breakout.

That is a clear short-term deterioration.

What makes it interesting is that the euro-side story actually improved this week.

Euro-area inflation accelerated to 3.3% in August from 2.9% in July. That has strengthened the case for another ECB rate increase in September, and policymakers had already indicated that July’s pause was probably not the end of tightening.

Normally, that should give the euro some support.

Instead, EUR/USD is struggling.

The reason is that the dollar has its own rate story. Markets are now pricing roughly a two-in-three chance of a Fed hike this month after Kevin Warsh’s Jackson Hole comments, while higher energy prices and elevated Treasury yields are keeping U.S. inflation concerns alive.

So this is not really a dovish-Fed versus hawkish-ECB setup anymore.

Both central banks are leaning toward tighter policy. The question is which side the data supports more.

What the chart shows

The failed breakout around 1.161–1.162 is now the first problem for buyers.

Price is trading beneath that area and sitting close to the rising trendline that has supported the recovery since July.

That trendline is the immediate test.

If it holds, the broader higher-low structure survives and another attempt to reclaim 1.162 remains possible.

If it breaks, the 1.150–1.152 demand zone becomes much more relevant.

Primary scenario

The near-term view stays cautious while EUR/USD remains below 1.162.

A break of rising support would make a deeper retest toward 1.150–1.152 consistent with the current structure.

Alternative scenario

If buyers defend the trendline and quickly reclaim 1.162, the latest weakness starts to look more like a failed breakdown than a genuine reversal.

That scenario would gain weight if U.S. labour data comes in soft enough to cool Fed hike expectations.

What comes next

The U.S. jobs report is the next real test.

The euro already has a more hawkish ECB behind it. What it does not have yet is price confirmation.

The ECB story improved, but EUR/USD is still asking buyers to prove it matters.</description><pubDate>Wed, 02 Sep 2026 19:32:34 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/EURUSD/DM8T78iQ-EUR-USD-Lost-the-Breakout-but-the-ECB-Is-Getting-More-Hawkish/</guid><content:encoded><![CDATA[
<div class="avatar">
	<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/u/Evelyn_Reed/" title="User Profile" target="_blank">
		<img src="https://s3.tradingview.com/userpics/144937190-8oXr_mid.png" width="32" height="32"/>
	</a>
</div>
<div class="chart-name">
	<h1>EUR/USD Lost the Breakout, but the ECB Is Getting More Hawkish</h1>
</div>

<div class="chart-symbol">
<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/symbols/EURUSD/">EUR/USD</a>	<span class="symbol-descr">OANDA:EURUSD</span>
</div>
<div class="chart-author">
	<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/u/Evelyn_Reed/" title="User Profile" target="_blank">Evelyn_Reed</a>
</div>

<a href="https://www.tradingview.com/chart/EURUSD/DM8T78iQ-EUR-USD-Lost-the-Breakout-but-the-ECB-Is-Getting-More-Hawkish/">
	<img src="https://s3.tradingview.com/d/DM8T78iQ_mid.png"/>
</a>
<p>EUR/USD is back below the 1.161–1.162 area after failing to hold the August breakout. <br/>
 <br/>
That is a clear short-term deterioration. <br/>
 <br/>
What makes it interesting is that the euro-side story actually improved this week. <br/>
 <br/>
Euro-area inflation accelerated to 3.3% in August from 2.9% in July. That has strengthened the case for another ECB rate increase in September, and policymakers had already indicated that July’s pause was probably not the end of tightening. <br/>
 <br/>
Normally, that should give the euro some support. <br/>
 <br/>
Instead, EUR/USD is struggling. <br/>
 <br/>
The reason is that the dollar has its own rate story. Markets are now pricing roughly a two-in-three chance of a Fed hike this month after Kevin Warsh’s Jackson Hole comments, while higher energy prices and elevated Treasury yields are keeping U.S. inflation concerns alive. <br/>
 <br/>
So this is not really a dovish-Fed versus hawkish-ECB setup anymore. <br/>
 <br/>
Both central banks are leaning toward tighter policy. The question is which side the data supports more. <br/>
 <br/>
What the chart shows <br/>
 <br/>
The failed breakout around 1.161–1.162 is now the first problem for buyers. <br/>
 <br/>
Price is trading beneath that area and sitting close to the rising trendline that has supported the recovery since July. <br/>
 <br/>
That trendline is the immediate test. <br/>
 <br/>
If it holds, the broader higher-low structure survives and another attempt to reclaim 1.162 remains possible. <br/>
 <br/>
If it breaks, the 1.150–1.152 demand zone becomes much more relevant. <br/>
 <br/>
Primary scenario <br/>
 <br/>
The near-term view stays cautious while EUR/USD remains below 1.162. <br/>
 <br/>
A break of rising support would make a deeper retest toward 1.150–1.152 consistent with the current structure. <br/>
 <br/>
Alternative scenario <br/>
 <br/>
If buyers defend the trendline and quickly reclaim 1.162, the latest weakness starts to look more like a failed breakdown than a genuine reversal. <br/>
 <br/>
That scenario would gain weight if U.S. labour data comes in soft enough to cool Fed hike expectations. <br/>
 <br/>
What comes next <br/>
 <br/>
The U.S. jobs report is the next real test. <br/>
 <br/>
The euro already has a more hawkish ECB behind it. What it does not have yet is price confirmation. <br/>
 <br/>
The ECB story improved, but EUR/USD is still asking buyers to prove it matters.</p>]]></content:encoded></item><item><title>check the trend </title><link>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/AUDUSD/d3r5RBxV-check-the-trend/</link><description>Considering the price reaction at the current resistance level, possible scenarios have been identified.
If the price breaks through the resistance level, the upward trend will likely continue.</description><pubDate>Wed, 02 Sep 2026 19:30:27 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.tradingview.com/chart/AUDUSD/d3r5RBxV-check-the-trend/</guid><content:encoded><![CDATA[
